  "body": "Node.js does not have access to what stdio (e.g. console) data goes to. Once it has gone through \"the pipe\" we are usually not able to alter it.\r\n\r\nCurrently Node.js has 4 possible output options for console (stdout/stderr) output:\r\n\r\n- Terminal (TTY)\r\n  - Only \"clear-able\" by control codes, sorta, in some terminals. Unreliable.\r\n- Pipe\r\n  - Dependant on the consumer, not possible.\r\n- File\r\n  - We _could_ clear the file, getting any of to agree to it will be a challenge, I think.\r\n- TCP Socket\r\n  - Yeah, you can actually have Node.js stdio (and console) write to / read from a TCP socket. I have no idea what implications this has.",
